Now let me carefully compare old vs new behavior. Key areas:

### 1. GitLab `fetchDiscussions` — `getPrComments` vs new `_gitlabFetchDiscussions`

Original ADO getPrComments:
```javascript
var parsed = _parseJson(ado_get_pr_comments({...}));
return (parsed && parsed.value) ? parsed.value : (parsed || []);
```
New: `_adoUnwrapList(...)`:
```javascript
var parsed = _parseJson(raw);
if (Array.isArray(parsed)) return parsed;
if (parsed && parsed.value) return parsed.value;
return parsed || [];
```
Wait — these differ! Original ADO getPrComments: `(parsed && parsed.value) ? parsed.value : (parsed || [])`. If parsed is an array → `parsed.value` is undefined → falls to `parsed || []` → returns the array. OK same result.

If parsed is null → `parsed || []` → []. New: parsed null → `parsed || []` → []. Same.
If parsed is an object without value → returns parsed (the object itself!) in original. New: same — `return parsed || []`.

Hmm, but ordering differs: new checks Array.isArray first. For an array with a `value` property (weird but possible — arrays are objects), original: `parsed.value` truthy → returns parsed.value. New: returns the array itself. Edge case, extremely unlikely. But `_adoUnwrapList` is also used for `listPrs`, whose original code was:
```javascript
var parsed = _parseJson(result);
if (Array.isArray(parsed)) return parsed;
if (parsed && parsed.value) return parsed.value;
return parsed || [];
```
That matches `_adoUnwrapList` exactly. And `getPrComments` original was `(parsed && parsed.value) ? parsed.value : (parsed || [])`. The only difference is the Array.isArray ordering. For an array that also has a `.value` property... arrays with a custom `value` property are basically never produced by JSON.parse (JSON can't create properties on arrays). So behaviorally identical for all JSON.parse outputs. OK — fine.

### 2. GitLab fetchDiscussions rendering — THE BIG ONE

Original:
```javascript
section += body ? ('**' + author + '** (' + date + '):\n' + body + '\n') : '_[No comment body]_\n';
for (var i = 1; i < notes.length; i++) {
    var reply = notes[i] || {};
    var rAuthor = reply.author ? (reply.author.username || reply.author.name) : 'unknown';
    var rDate = reply.created_at ? reply.created_at.substring(0, 10) : '';
    section += '> **' + rAuthor + '** (' + rDate + '): ' + (reply.body || '').trim() + '\n';
}
section += '---\n';
```

New:
```javascript
section += mapped.body
    ? ('**' + author + '** (' + date + '):\n' + mapped.body + '\n')
    : '_[No comment body]_\n';
for (var i = 1; i < notes.length; i++) {
    var reply = notes[i] || {};
    section += '> **' + rAuthor + '** (' + rDate + '): ' + (reply.body || '').trim() + '\n';
}
section += '---\n';
```

CONFIRMED BEHAVIOR CHANGE: 
- body line: `\n` → `\n` (blank line after body removed)
- reply lines: `\n` → `\n` (blank line after each reply removed)
- `---` line: `\n` → `\n` (blank line before `---` removed)

This changes the rendered markdown: previously replies were separated by blank lines (each rendered as its own blockquote paragraph in Markdown); now they're consecutive lines, which in Markdown merges into a single blockquote paragraph — actually for blockquotes, consecutive `> ` lines merge into ONE blockquote with a soft line break. Previously blank line between them would merge into one blockquote too (blank quote line `>` omitted → lazy continuation). Hmm, in Markdown, `> a\n> b` renders as one paragraph "a b" (soft break); `> a\n> b` renders as two separate blockquotes. So the rendering changes from two separate blockquote blocks to one merged paragraph.

Also body followed by reply: previously blank line separated body paragraph and blockquote; now the `> reply` line directly follows the body line — the body is a paragraph and `>` starts a blockquote; without a blank line, `> reply` after a paragraph line... in CommonMark, a `>` line following a paragraph line starts... Actually a blockquote CAN interrupt a paragraph in CommonMark. So rendering differs subtly.

